Chamfers: a chamfer wider than a face of its edges along part of its
chain, the faces beyond that face growing down to it, and a chamfer's end
where its contour was stopped at a turn (mitcad#263).

The case: the tip edges of a fin 1 mm thick chamfered by 0.263 at 45
degrees, the fin's root chamfered (a cone at 40 degrees to the fin's
side) so wide that the strip of the side between the root's chamfer and
the tip is 0.16 to 0.31 mm high: narrower than the chamfer along most of
its straight stretches, wider along its arcs. The walk reached the strip's
far edge and would roll on it, which chamfers do not ("PerformSurf Not
Implemented"). The material the chamfer takes away is the triangle of its
section less the part inside the root's chamfer extended: along the arcs
the chamfer's cone with a strip of the side above it; along the stretches
no chamfer face, the root's cone running on up to the tip; between them
the chamfer cut by the root's cone across its width.

- ChFi3d_Builder::ChamferOverNarrowFace: where a chamfer's computation
  fails, or a walked chamfer's contact left its face across an edge of
  that face (not of the spine) into a face beyond at an angle within the
  spine while the walk went on, the chamfer is computed again with that
  face B's surface unbounded (ConexFaces gives an unbounded face for B
  while it is computed, ChFi3d_NarrowFaces; past the spine's ends a walk
  that fails, or a twisted piece, is left out), and then:
- ChFi3d_Builder::ChamferCutByFacesBeyond: where the contact on B leaves B
  and comes back (or reaches an end of the spine) is found on the
  contact's line, with B's edge crossed and the face C beyond it. Where
  the chamfer's other contact lies outside C's material there, C cuts the
  chamfer from the crossing back across it to the other contact (at K, a
  new vertex): the piece up to K is the chamfer as walked, the piece from
  K to the crossing has the cut on C as its side (ChFi3d_NarrowCutPiece,
  ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve), and ends in a point on B's edge; past it to the
  next coming back nothing of the chamfer is left. The parts kept are
  stripes of their own (myNarrowParts, added once the stripes are
  computed), each ending in points where it is cut off; the part at an
  end of the spine that is not cut off keeps that end and its corner.
- The faces beyond grow down to the chamfer's other face A: the curve of A
  and C from one K to the next bounds both (a new curve of the data
  structure on both faces, each on the side it keeps: across a convex
  crease the part inside the other's material, across a concave one the
  part outside it); where the part cut off reaches the spine's end, B's
  edges from the crossing to the spine's end vertex give the faces beyond
  in turn, the edge between two of them is prolonged along its tangent
  from its vertex on B to A (a new vertex where A, C and C' meet), the
  curves of A with each face beyond run between those points and the
  spine's end vertex, and B's edges and the spine's edges under the part
  cut off bound no face (cut at their vertices with no side kept). A
  face beyond that is a B-spline (a walked chamfer) is taken with its
  sections continued straight past its bounds (ChFi3d_WideSurface: its
  directions of degree 1 extended, the parameters kept).
- Where the contact on B runs past the face at an end of the spine, or
  reaches the spine's end on an edge of B there, its point is put on
  that edge, as the walk on B gives it, and the corner there is made as
  before. ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfSurf does not cut such a chamfer's
  ends by the faces at the ends again (WalkedCutToEnd, RolledCutToEnd)
  where it ends in a point, and a try that fails leaves it as it was.
- ChFi3d_Builder::ChamferEndPastTurnStop: a chamfer whose contour was
  stopped at a turn (StopChainsAtTurns, now also BRepFilletAPI_MakeChamfer's)
  at the vertex V at an end of the spine, where the end edge's analytic
  blend has its contact on face N ending on N's edge into the face F0
  beyond the crease, which holds V: that blend runs on past V, cut by F0,
  up to where its other contact reaches F0 on the edge of its face with
  F0 at V, and the stripe ends in a point there (no walk past V, no
  corner). Before, the walk past V and the corner there made a patch of
  their own.

Upstream: the new paths are taken only where the chamfer failed before, or
where its walk left a face mid-spine as described; a chamfer that is not
cut that way is computed again as before.

